        "summary":  "A study by researchers from Harvard Business School, MIT, and the University of Washington found that AI-generated explanations for recommendations can suppress independent human judgment in innovation screening. Counterintuitively, black-box AI recommendations without narrative explanations led to better decision-making than those accompanied by explanations, as detailed rationales encouraged evaluators to defer to AI rather than verify outputs independently. The researchers advise enterprises to design AI systems that preserve human discretion, particularly in early-stage screening contexts.",
        "execSummary":  "Enterprise leaders deploying AI decision-support tools face a hidden risk: the more transparent and explanatory an AI system appears, the more likely human reviewers are to abandon independent thinking and simply defer to the machine. Harvard, MIT, and UW research shows that AI-generated rationales suppress productive human disagreement — meaning well-intentioned explainability features can paradoxically degrade decision quality at the moment it matters most. For customer-support and operations leaders scaling AI guidance tools, this reframes the design question from \u0027how do we make AI more transparent?\u0027 to \u0027how do we preserve human override behavior?\u0027 The implication is clear: audit your AI workflows for deference traps before you scale them.",
